---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { evidenceRegistry, parameterRegistry } from '../../lib/cortex/evidence.js';
---
<Layout pageTitle="Cortex Laboratory — methods" subtitle="What is calculated, what is assumed, and what remains unvalidated.">
  <article class="cortex-methods">
    <a href="/cortex/">← Open the laboratory</a>
    <h2>A verified mathematical foundation</h2>
    <p>The current laboratory solves a passive, sealed, branched cable and calculates extracellular potentials from its total transmembrane currents. Numerical checks establish correctness for stated mathematical cases. No experimental dataset has yet validated this implementation.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Subsystem</th><th>Current status</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Implant placement</td><td>Implemented; numerically verified</td><td>Shared catalog dimensions and rigid transforms. Tip exposure remains a reconstruction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Passive cable and volume conductor</td><td>Numerically verified; exploratory biology</td><td>Analytical tests and refinement checks, without fitted human physiology.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cortical anatomy</td><td>Representative geometry</td><td>Planar layers, one synthetic arbor, no population density or scan reconstruction.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Active channels, recruitment, acquisition, mechanics</td><td>Not yet modeled here</td><td>No spike, clinical, tissue-safety or deformation predictions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Experimental validation</td><td>Not yet performed</td><td>No independent experimental comparison or calibration has been completed.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <h2>One coordinate system</h2>
    <p>Positions use micrometers in a right-handed frame: pia is Z = 0; positive Z points into cortex. Rendering converts every structure uniformly to millimeters. Implant rotations apply X, then Y, then Z about the substrate center, followed by translation. The scale bar measures distance in the camera-target plane; the coordinate inspector gives exact model coordinates and soma distances.</p>
    <p>The Utah 1.5 mm reference comes directly from the existing device catalog. Its 100 physical shanks and 96 nominal channels are separate fields. The chosen sensing sites are hypothetical ideal probes, not an invented wiring map. Unknown exposed areas and channel assignments stay null. Neither substrate displacement nor electrical contrast is modeled.</p>
    <h2>Membrane and extracellular currents</h2>
    <p>For compartment i, C is total capacitance, gₗ is leak conductance, Gᵢⱼ is axial conductance and Vₘ = Vᵢ − φₑ. Positive membrane current points outward. The solver uses mV, ms, nA, nF and µS, with explicit conversions from membrane area and resistivity.</p>
    <pre>Cᵢ dVₘ,ᵢ/dt + gₗ,ᵢ(Vₘ,ᵢ − Eₗ) + gₛ,ᵢ(Vₘ,ᵢ − Eₛ)
  = Σⱼ Gᵢⱼ [(Vₘ,ⱼ + φₑ,ⱼ) − (Vₘ,ᵢ + φₑ,ᵢ)] + Iᵢₙⱼ,ᵢ

Iₘ,ᵢ = Cᵢ dVₘ,ᵢ/dt + Iₗₑₐₖ,ᵢ + Iₛᵧₙ,ᵢ
Σᵢ Iₘ,ᵢ = Σᵢ Iᵢₙⱼ,ᵢ</pre>
    <p>Backward Euler and tree elimination solve the linear membrane system. The initial state equals leak equilibrium. Cable tips are sealed. Synaptic input is a prescribed conductance step spread over a fixed distal 100 µm of the apical branch, so refining the mesh preserves the input region. The soma uses a spherical membrane area and an equivalent cylindrical axial connection; branching and dimensions are assumptions.</p>
    <p>The extracellular point-source solution in an infinite isotropic conductor is φ = Σ Iₘ/(4πσr). The line-source option integrates 1/r uniformly along each cylindrical compartment and uses a point source for the soma. Tissue potentials are referred to infinity or differenced as A − B. The code rejects selected sources or sensing points within 25 µm of the modeled membrane instead of hiding the singularity with a distance clamp. That rule does not prove accuracy outside the excluded region.</p>
    <h2>Three distinct interventions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synaptic recording:</strong> a conductance event produces passive currents and an extracellular signal. Total transmembrane current sums to zero.</li>
      <li><strong>Intracellular current clamp:</strong> current enters the soma; an explicit bath return contributes both an applied field and a recording artifact. Net membrane current equals the injected current.</li>
      <li><strong>Extracellular polarization:</strong> two ideal point contacts deliver equal and opposite biphasic currents. The resulting extracellular gradient drives the same cable. A uniform potential offset cannot alter its membrane response.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The neural contribution, applied-source artifact and their sum remain separate. Stimulation and recording cannot use the same ideal source point because its potential is undefined there. Charge per phase is descriptive. Exposed contact area is unknown, so geometric charge density is not supplied.</p>
    <h2>Parameters and evidence</h2>
    <p>These are defaults, not empirical confidence intervals or human M1 population means. The canonical membrane time constant is 20 ms. User-edited configurations and actual units are preserved in every result bundle.</p>
    <table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>Evidence category / limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>{parameterRegistry.map(p => <tr><td>{p.id}</td><td>{p.value} {p.unit}</td><td>{p.category}. {p.limitation}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
    <p>The cortical slab is 6 × 6 × 4 mm by default. Layer fractions are 0, 0.06, 0.16, 0.46, 0.50, 0.75 and 1 times thickness. These boundaries, including layer IV, are illustrative. The displayed CSF and white matter do not create different conductivities. Curvature, vessels, meningeal mechanics, neuronal density and excitatory/inhibitory populations are absent.</p>
    <h2>Verification, exports and performance</h2>
    <p>Reproducible tests check SI unit conversions, line-source quadrature, dipole decay, analytic RC and two-compartment responses, sealed-cable polarization, current conservation, gauge invariance, rigid transforms, charge balance, singularity rejection, deterministic runs and exact CSV/JSON agreement. The default recording experiment changes by less than 1% in peak membrane excursion and peak neural potential under the tested separate timestep and spatial refinements. This is not a universal error bound.</p>
    <p>An independent NEURON 9.0.1 reference was also run: a sealed 41-segment passive cylinder under intracellular current injection. At ten sampled frames, the browser kernel agreed within 8 × 10⁻¹³ mV in voltage and 1.4 × 10⁻¹⁵ nA in membrane current. This checks one numerical case, not the full cortical arbor or experimental biology. The generator, package versions and hashed fixture are retained in the repository.</p>
    <p>Run <code>node --test tests/cortex-laboratory.mjs</code> and <code>node scripts/benchmark-cortex.mjs</code> in the repository. The benchmark report records hardware, timings, resolution and convergence. The on-page timer measures the local numerical run, not rendering FPS. Cross-browser speed, full peak memory and GPU performance have not been measured.</p>
    <p>JSON bundles include the configuration, engine and catalog revisions, geometry, source registry, frame-major compartment voltages/currents, separated potentials and numerical diagnostics. CSV exports the selected signal component plus every compartment current. Frame zero is equilibrium; later samples are step-end values, paired with the average drive over the preceding interval. No analog filtering, anti-aliasing or ADC is implied. Configuration JSON can be imported; the format is our versioned schema, not SWC, SONATA, NeuroML or NWB.</p>
    <h2>Primary sources and scope</h2>
    <ol class="sources">{evidenceRegistry.sources.map(s => <li id={s.id}><a href={s.url}>{s.authors}{s.year ? ` (${s.year})` : ''}: {s.title}</a><p>{s.locator}. {s.doi ? `DOI: ${s.doi}.` : ''} Evidence: {s.species}. {s.limitation}</p></li>)}</ol>
    <h2>Next scientific gate</h2>
    <p>Reproduce a matched NEURON/LFPy reference, select a licensed region-specific morphology and independent recordings, then introduce active channels. Human action-potential thresholds, multilayer finite-element fields and mechanics require their own validation. The existing legacy tools remain separate until their models can meet the shared geometry and current contracts.</p>
    <p><a href="/cortex/">Return to the laboratory →</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>
<style>
  .cortex-methods{max-width:900px;margin:24px auto;line-height:1.7;font-size:15px}.cortex-methods h2{font-size:26px;font-weight:400;margin-top:34px}.cortex-methods a{color:inherit}.cortex-methods table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px;margin:20px 0}.cortex-methods td,.cortex-methods th{text-align:left;vertical-align:top;padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)}.cortex-methods pre{font-size:12px;line-height:1.7;padding:20px;background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--border);overflow:auto}.cortex-methods code{font-size:12px}.sources li{margin-bottom:18px}.sources p{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);margin:5px 0}@media(max-width:650px){.cortex-methods{font-size:14px}.cortex-methods table{font-size:11px}.cortex-methods td,.cortex-methods th{padding:7px 4px}}
</style>
